14:56:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It shows that changing her diet may have helped her headaches, and that is important for her. But it does not prove that all additives—or even one specific “chemical”—caused them. Cutting out processed food often changes many things at once: less alcohol, caffeine, salty foods, processed meats, artificial sweeteners, skipped meals, dehydration, blood-sugar swings, or certain personal trigger foods. Any of those can affect headaches or migraines. A stronger way to identify a trigger is to reintroduce one suspected food or ingredient at a time, ideally with a food-and-headache diary and guidance from a clinician if headaches are chronic. If headaches return reliably after a particular product and improve when it is removed, that is useful evidence for *her*—even if it does not mean the ingredient harms everyone. So the practical conclusion is fair: if avoiding processed foods stopped her headaches, she should probably keep avoiding the foods that triggered them. For new, severe, sudden, worsening, or neurologically unusual headaches, medical evaluation matters.
